x}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b,$ then what is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186240992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d52be40-210e-5024-9a79-b6ca4323223c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240992 #edb/ref 1074 "If ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=b$, then we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 3{x}_{1} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ -8{x}_{2} \\\\ 6{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve this system using Gaussian elimination. First, we write the augmented matrix for this system:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the augmented matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 6 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 6 & -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=-5 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=6.$ Substituting ${x}_{3}=6$ into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-6=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{3}=6$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(1)+2(6)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-7.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution of the system is ${x}_{1}=-7$, ${x}_{2}=1$, and ${x}_{3}=6,$ which means \n\n$$\n-7{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+6{a}_{3}=b.\n$$\n\nConsequently, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-7+1+6=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186241751 #edb/ref 1080 "y-(4+\\text{i})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241751 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241751 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3f8ba98b-b635-57cd-820c-5d662868757d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242284 #edb/ref 1080 "y-(3+2\\text{i})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242284 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242284 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7ddb64c3-6920-561d-85f7-cb1a72153226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242431 #edb/ref 1080 "y-(2+3\\text{i})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242431 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242431 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e557471-c17d-509e-b5b0-c916eeb42439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242644 #edb/ref 1080 "y-(1-4\\text{i})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242644 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242644 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a64c07a4-adf4-5664-bb94-d04a1e47b64e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242711 #edb/ref 1080 "y-(3+4\\text{i})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242711 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186242711 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "afcf5b7a-3cfd-5db2-9715-1a947fbc2372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243613 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243613 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243613 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243613 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "56e5fdb7-c6cf-585e-a066-ab65dcd10706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244102 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a factor of ${y}^{2}-8y+17?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "963cb1b5-4672-5772-af3f-0514a1529c6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244102 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we let $f(y)={y}^{2}-8y+17.$\n\nNext, we solve $f(y)=0$ by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-8y+17 & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-8y+{4}^{2})-{4}^{2}+17 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2}-16+17 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & ={\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =(\\text{i})^{2} \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our quadratic equation has roots ${y}_{1}=4+\\text{i}$ and ${y}_{2}=4-\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=1,$ the expression ${y}^{2}-8y+17$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8y+17 & =a(y-{y}_{1})(y-{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(y-(4+\\text{i}))(y-(4-\\text{i})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, among the given options, only $y-(4+\\text{i})$ is a factor of ${y}^{2}-8y+17.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244115 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449015 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244115 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449016 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244115 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449017 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186244115 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244115 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244115 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n\n- **II.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186244115 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fa81e71-e4f5-5405-bfdd-41cd7387b276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244115 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $-1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-2v)=-2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (but not $-6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186247830 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{9\\pi +4}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247830 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247830 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ddafc2d-893b-58b6-93dd-9b191a92a685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247841 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247841 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247841 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2eb658f2-fe35-5e10-bef3-933961176bf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248114 #edb/ref 1080 "12(3\\pi +2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248114 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248114 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4cfe36d1-77cf-5186-80e9-ceac37ea2ad7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248480 #edb/ref 1080 "8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248480 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248480 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7823881d-7e2c-52ef-b688-59e51a932f84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248522 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3\\pi +2}{6}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248522 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186248522 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7cfd92ca-b4c4-57ee-a746-bf891b259937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249144 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249144 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249144 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c1414e3b-0d2d-575e-aec1-cf23894cfc17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186249371 #e